WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:16.460
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: I think most of the substantive
questions we are covering later on in the

00:00:16.470 --> 00:00:23.880
agenda, so I will just supplement that by
recording that I have, as is usually the case,

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:31.410
done a number of parliamentary committee hearings
recently, and in particular have underlined

00:00:31.410 --> 00:00:37.530
the focus on social care, which has been the
subject of enquiry both in the House of Lords

00:00:37.530 --> 00:00:43.760
on Monday and the House of Commons yesterday.
The points that I have been making on those

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:48.490
occasions have been that, when we set out
the Five Year Forward View, we were, I think,

00:00:48.490 --> 00:00:55.200
crystal clear that in order for the NHS to
be able to bring about the improvements we

00:00:55.200 --> 00:01:00.079
all want to see, we do need a well-resourced
social care system that is keeping up with

00:01:00.079 --> 00:01:09.150
increased social care demand. I think the
pressures are obvious in terms of the rising

00:01:09.150 --> 00:01:15.310
number, particularly of frail, older people,
who find themselves in hospital when they

00:01:15.310 --> 00:01:21.850
and their families would like to be able to
come home. We have seen in the last three

00:01:21.850 --> 00:01:29.990
years a doubling in the number of formally
measured delayed‑discharge days in hospital,

00:01:29.990 --> 00:01:37.990
equivalent to about 6,500 hospital beds at
any one time being occupied by people who

00:01:37.990 --> 00:01:44.490
are ready to go home. That is probably a low-end
estimate; it is just the way the official

00:01:44.490 --> 00:01:50.920
measure works. When you look at the way that
measure is constructed, we can now see that

00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:57.009
the majority of delayed discharges are actually
in some way or another attributable to the

00:01:57.009 --> 00:02:01.320
availability, or lack thereof, of social care
services.

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:06.771
It is worth just remembering that about two
thirds of hospital inpatients are over the

00:02:06.771 --> 00:02:15.249
age of 65, and more than a quarter of hospital
inpatients have dementia. We are serving many

00:02:15.249 --> 00:02:20.140
of the same people in different parts of the
public service, and this obviously represents

00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:26.079
huge change since the NHS was founded; in
1948, half of people in this country died

00:02:26.079 --> 00:02:33.840
before the age of 50. Now, a third of children
born are likely to be living to more than

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:38.560
100, so these are pressures that are only
going to increase. We are going to have a

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:42.969
million more people over the age of 75 by
the end of this parliament than at the beginning,

00:02:42.969 --> 00:02:48.559
and that is why the stance that we have been
taking is to say: our view is that we need

00:02:48.559 --> 00:02:54.639
a three-part approach to sorting out the pressures
on social care. First, that there needs to

00:02:54.639 --> 00:03:02.079
be immediate financial support, and that,
were extra funding to be available, social

00:03:02.079 --> 00:03:09.620
care should be front of the queue. The Local
Government Association has estimated that

00:03:09.620 --> 00:03:16.900
the adult social care funding gap for next
year, 2017/2018, is around £1.3 billion.

00:03:16.900 --> 00:03:22.000
Other independent estimates put it at perhaps
£1.9 billion; people like the King’s Fund,

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:28.439
the Health Foundation and the Nuffield Trust.
So, obviously, the increases that will be

00:03:28.439 --> 00:03:35.909
raised through the social care precept are
a contribution to that, but will not wholly

00:03:35.909 --> 00:03:43.430
by any means close that gap. The first step
has to be some immediate support. The second

00:03:43.430 --> 00:03:49.069
has to be that, as we roll out the STP process
through the country, we have to have better

00:03:49.069 --> 00:03:54.639
health and social care integration in care
delivery and assessment. The third is that,

00:03:54.639 --> 00:04:02.869
in my opinion, we will need, post-2020, a
wider debate on security in old age, and that

00:04:02.869 --> 00:04:07.959
will need to look at a more profound set of
changes to the way social care is financed,

00:04:07.959 --> 00:04:12.469
alongside other aspects of the support that
older people get.

00:04:12.469 --> 00:04:18.160
I think increasingly, this agenda is now being
recognised. I said yesterday that I felt that

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:23.240
the country had moved from denial to acknowledgement
of the fact that there is an issue, and obviously

00:04:23.240 --> 00:04:27.669
now we need to move from acknowledgement to
action on those three fronts.

00:04:27.669 --> 00:04:34.389
The only other point I think I would say,
by way of getting us kicked off, is a lot

00:04:34.389 --> 00:04:40.229
of our discussions are obviously focused on
the pressures in A&amp;E departments, the operational

00:04:40.229 --> 00:04:46.150
here and now. We sometimes forget that the
Health Service is a very comprehensive set

00:04:46.150 --> 00:04:51.430
of services, and on the discussion we are
going to be having with Cally on cancer care,

00:04:51.430 --> 00:04:58.430
for example: the cancer one-year survival
rate figures were published a fortnight ago,

00:04:58.430 --> 00:05:03.069
and received no coverage whatsoever. What
they actually show is that the quality of

00:05:03.069 --> 00:05:09.609
cancer care in the NHS has improved again
over the course of the last year, so that

00:05:09.609 --> 00:05:15.000
2,400 families around the country will have
a loved one with them this Christmas who would

00:05:15.010 --> 00:05:20.080
not have been alive last Christmas. On any
of the major measures you look, you can see

00:05:20.080 --> 00:05:22.870
those kinds of gains.

00:05:22.870 --> 00:05:27.629
In the spirit of your conversation, Malcolm,
about joint work with other agencies, the

00:05:27.629 --> 00:05:32.729
Board of the Human Fertilisation and Embryology
Authority is meeting this morning to consider

00:05:32.729 --> 00:05:39.340
whether to begin accepting licences for a
groundbreaking new treatment called mitochondrial

00:05:39.340 --> 00:05:45.919
donation, and in the event that they decide
that they will accept those, then we at NHS

00:05:45.919 --> 00:05:51.509
England are this morning committing to funding
up to £8 million worth for a clinical trial

00:05:51.509 --> 00:05:57.600
over the next five years, which will be a
world-first, and will produce potentially

00:05:57.600 --> 00:06:04.939
huge benefits for mums, in which they will
get a form of IVF which the future baby’s

00:06:04.939 --> 00:06:09.729
mitochondrial DNA comes from a donor egg,
so they do not pass on the inherited condition;

00:06:09.729 --> 00:06:14.770
just another example of the kinds of innovations
that the NHS is actually getting on with,

00:06:14.770 --> 00:06:21.919
building on our heritage as being the world
leader on modern cataract surgery, hip replacements,

00:06:21.920 --> 00:06:26.660
vaccines, other cancer treatments and so on,
and so we must not forget that, as well.

00:06:26.660 --> 00:06:31.680
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Simon, thank you.  May I invite any questions or comments on the Chief Executive’s Report?

00:06:32.720 --> 00:06:34.720
No?  Well, thank you very much for that.

